# KNOWN GAP — 🚨 a `Vector[Box[Trait]]` built with `Box.new` + `push` and
# returned from a helper SEGFAULTS: rc 139 on both backends, ASan
# `stack-buffer-overflow` in `__interceptor_memcpy`.
#
# ⚠ DISCRIMINATED FROM ITS SIBLING BY FORMATION SPELLING — do not merge them.
# The same program written with the CTOR CALL and a CONTAINER LITERAL --
# `Box(Robot(...))` + `Vector[Box[Speaker]] v = [b]` -- fails DIFFERENTLY at
# HEAD: rc 0 printing the EMPTY STRING on both lanes, ASan
# `heap-use-after-free` in `gorget_string_clone_to_owned`. That sibling is
# REPAIRED by R45 Track A's fix B (measured: rc 0 with the correct string,
# leaving a 98-byte leak that is filed as `t0700`).
#
# THIS spelling -- `Box.new(...)` + `v.push(b)` -- is rc 139 at HEAD **and
# still rc 139 under fix A + fix B** (measured 2026-08-27 with the patch
# applied). So it is not the same defect wearing a different costume: it
# survives the repair that closes the sibling.
#
# The two differ in exactly two tokens, which is why this is worth pinning:
#   sibling : Box(Robot(mk(..)))      + Vector[Box[Speaker]] v = [b]
#   THIS    : Box.new(Robot(mk(..)))  + v.push(b)
#
# ⚠ The payload is heap-forced through `mk`. A literal would not reallocate
# and would hide the defect.
#
# INTENDED: prints `R2` on both backends, ASan-clean.
#
# RED-verified 2026-08-27 at pristine HEAD (rc 139, C and LLVM) and again
# under Track A's fix A+B patch (still rc 139), by the R45 orchestrator while
# verifying Track A brief-review pass 18's P18-2.
from std.collections import Box
trait Speaker:
    String speak(self)
struct Robot:
    String id
equip Robot with Speaker:
    String speak(self):
        return self.id

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

Vector[Box[Speaker]] make():
    Box[Robot] b = Box.new(Robot(mk("R", "2")))
    Vector[Box[Speaker]] v = []
    v.push(b)
    return v

void main():
    Vector[Box[Speaker]] v = make()
    print(v.get(0).unwrap().speak())
